course: everyday
lesson: 471
title: The kid who has it in for you
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In Year 2 Sam knocked over Dean's Lego tower by accident. Three years later Dean still «has it in for him», which means he keeps picking on him on purpose: «Oi, Sam-wise, still crying?», a shove in the line, Sam's bag kicked under the radiator. The day the bag ends up in a puddle, Sam tells his teacher. The teacher talks to Dean and his parents. It doesn't stop overnight, but it gets much better.
explain: An enemy at school can look bigger than he is. Ellie tells everyone Maddie smells «like old soup», and Maddie sniffs her jumper in the toilets for a week, until her mum hugs her: «You don't smell, love. She's being horrible.» Insults are often lies. Bella tells the class that Chloe's dad is in prison; really he works on oil rigs in the North Sea and is away for weeks at a time. Mean rumours grow as they spread. Arjun keeps staring at Lily and whispering, and Lily is sure he's plotting, until his mate says, «He fancies you, he's just a bit of a muppet about it.» Not every starer is an enemy.
explain: Revenge makes two people wrong. Kayla sticks her foot out at Mia three times; the third time Mia trips her back, and Kayla hits her head on the table. Both go to the head, both get a phone call home. When Tyler whispers «You're dead at break», Connor tells his teacher before break, and Tyler never says it again. Telling about a threat isn't grassing (telling tales), it's safety.
explain: The strangest turns come last. Dean falls off the climbing frame holding his wrist, everyone stares, and Sam helps him up and walks him to the office. Next day Dean mutters «ta» (thanks) and leaves him alone. Jake and Kieran have been enemies since nursery, and neither of them can remember why. Kieran's grandad says, «Hate's heavy, son. Too heavy for a lad your size to carry about.» And when her rival Ellie wins the writing prize, Maddie wants to say «you only won 'cos Miss likes you» — but she tells Ellie, «Well done, it was good.» Ellie blinks: «Yours was good too.»
fact: A grudge is a bad feeling kept for a long time; some grudges start with a tiny accident and last for years.
fact: When a child keeps picking on you, telling a teacher or parent is not telling tales; it is how it gets stopped.
fact: Insults are often simply lies said to make someone feel small; checking with someone who loves you helps.
fact: Not everyone who stares or whispers is an enemy; it is wise not to assume the worst.
fact: Getting your own back usually lands both children in trouble, and someone can really get hurt.
fact: Any threat, such as «you're dead at break», should be told to a grown-up straight away, whoever says it.
fact: A rumour is a story passed from person to person; mean rumours are often untrue and grow as they spread.
fact: Helping someone who is hurt is always right, even if that person has been unkind to you.
fact: Kindness to an enemy can change how they treat you, though it does not always work.
fact: Hating someone mostly weighs on the person doing the hating; you can dislike someone without thinking about them all day.
fact: Being polite to a rival who wins, saying «well done», is a sign of strength, not weakness.
fact: «Grassing» is British slang for telling on someone; «ta» means thanks; «muppet» means a silly person.
exercise: Why did Dean have it in for Sam? | Sam knocked his Lego tower over by accident in Year 2 | Sam stole his bag in Year 4 and threw it in a puddle at the bus stop | Sam called him names every day | Sam beat him in a race last week
exercise: What did Sam finally do when his bag ended up in a puddle? | Told his teacher | Threw Dean's bag in the puddle | Kept quiet for another year | Moved to another school
exercise: After the teacher spoke to Dean and his parents, what happened? | It got much better, though not overnight | It stopped forever on the very same day, like magic | Nothing changed at all | Sam got the blame
exercise: Ellie says Maddie smells «like old soup». What is it really? | A cruel lie to make Maddie feel small | A helpful health tip from a friend who cares | A true fact Maddie should hide | A friendly joke between mates
exercise: Arjun kept staring at Lily and whispering. What was really going on? | He fancied her and was awkward about it | He was planning to hurt her at break time | He wanted to copy all her work in maths | He was telling everyone all of her secrets
exercise: Jake and Kieran have been enemies since nursery. Why? | Nobody can remember any more | Kieran broke Jake's bike on purpose | Jake told lies about Kieran's mum | They both wanted the same seat
exercise: Mia tripped Kayla back and Kayla hit her head. What happened next? | Both were sent to the head and their parents called | Only Kayla got into trouble, because she had started it | Mia got a prize for standing up for herself | The teacher never found out
exercise: Tyler whispered «You're dead at break». What did Connor do? | Told his teacher before break | Brought a big stick to school for break | Hid in the toilets all break | Said «you're dead» back
exercise: Why is telling a teacher about a threat not grassing? | It's about keeping people safe | Teachers like hearing gossip | Threats are only jokes anyway | It is grassing, so don't do it
exercise: Bella said Chloe's dad was in prison. Where does he really work? | On oil rigs in the North Sea | In a prison as a guard | Nowhere, he has no job | In a shop next to the school
exercise: What did Sam do when Dean hurt his wrist? | Helped him up and took him to the office | Laughed with everyone else | Walked away, because Dean deserved it after all | Took a photo for the class chat
exercise: What did Kieran's grandad mean by «Hate's heavy, son»? | Hating someone mostly hurts the one who hates | Hate makes you put on weight | Kieran should hate Jake even more, and show it to him | Only grown-ups are allowed to hate
exercise: Maddie's rival won the writing prize. What did Maddie say? | «Well done, it was good» | «You only won because Miss likes you» | «Your story was rubbish anyway» | Nothing, she walked out of the room
exercise: How did Ellie answer Maddie's «well done»? | «Yours was good too» | «I know, I'm the best» | «Shut up, you don't mean it» | She burst into tears and ran off
exercise: What does «ta» mean when Dean mutters it? | Thanks | Go away | Goodbye forever | Watch out
exercise: Which is the wisest when a kid has it in for you for months? | Tell a grown-up you trust | Get your own back secretly | Put up with it, it'll stop | Pick on someone smaller
test: «Dean's been shoving me since Year 2 lol, it's just what he does». Should Sam just put up with it? | No, tell a teacher, that's how it stops | Yes, grudges always fade on their own | Yes, telling makes it worse every time | No, shove him back harder
test: Maddie, sniffing her jumper: «do i actually smell?? Ellie said». What helps most? | Asking someone who loves her and telling a teacher | Washing everything three times a day so Ellie stops saying it | Never going to school again | Saying Ellie smells worse
test: «that lad keeps lookin at me, he's deffo plottin summat, no cap». Is that sure? | No, staring doesn't prove he's an enemy | Yes, staring always means trouble is coming soon | Yes, hit him first to be safe | No, he's definitely her best friend
test: Kayla trips Mia for the third time. What would most likely happen if Mia tells the teacher instead of tripping back? | Kayla gets sorted and nobody gets hurt | Mia gets the blame for telling | Kayla would trip her ten more times | Nothing, teachers never help
test: «Tyler says I'm dead at break, but he's always messin, innit». What should Connor do? | Tell a teacher before break, just in case | Ignore it, he's always messing, so it's just a joke | Bring his big brother to fight | Hide in the toilets forever
test: «heard chloe's dad's in jail, what'd he do??» What's the best answer? | Don't pass on rumours, ask a teacher if worried | Ask Chloe loudly in front of the class so it's cleared up | Pass it on so everyone knows | Make up a crime he did
test: Dean's crying with his wrist bent. A kid says, «leave him, he's a proper mardy bully». («mardy» means moody and sulky.) What's right? | Help him or get a grown-up anyway | Leave him, he deserves it | Film it for a laugh | Push him over again
test: Kieran: «I proper hate Jake, I'm gonna hate him forever». What did Grandad suggest? | That hate is a heavy thing to carry around | That he should get Jake back first, before Jake gets him | That hating is good for you | That he should never speak again
test: In a cartoon, the hero gets revenge and everyone cheers. In real life, what usually happens after revenge? | Both kids end up in trouble | Everybody cheers like in the cartoon | The enemy becomes a friend at once | Nobody ever finds out about it
test: «why'd u say well done to Ellie, she's ur enemy, that's sus». Why was it a good move? | Being gracious to a rival is a strength | Maddie wanted Ellie's prize money | Maddie was scared of Ellie | It wasn't, she should have sulked
test: Which of these is true about old grudges? | They can last years, even from a tiny accident | They always end after a week | Only grown-ups have them | They only ever start from big, nasty fights with punches
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of a pupil who reports to a teacher a threat of violence made against him? | He acts prudently in the interest of his own safety | He commits an act of disloyalty deserving censure | He exaggerates a matter of no consequence | He thereby renders the threat more likely to be executed
exam: Identify the principal consequence, as established in the lesson, of retaliation by one pupil against another who has repeatedly tripped her. | Both pupils incur disciplinary consequences and injury may result | The original offender alone is punished | The matter is concluded to universal satisfaction | The retaliating pupil receives commendation
exam: Ascertain the most appropriate conduct of a pupil upon observing that a peer who has persistently mistreated him has sustained an injury. | To render assistance and convey him to an adult | To leave him unattended in consequence of his conduct | To record the incident for circulation among peers | To inform peers of his discomfiture
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning derogatory remarks made by one pupil about another. | Such remarks are frequently untrue and intended to diminish the recipient | Such remarks are invariably accurate | Such remarks ought to be accepted without inquiry | Such remarks constitute friendly counsel
